The Great Agent Sign-In Pro= blem.


Imagine 10,000 AI agents signing into your pro= duct. Would you celebrate, or call your security team?

Those could be agents working for thousands of= customers who want to use your product. Or one person creating thousands o= f accounts to burn through your free tier. You would want to know who was b= ehind them before you called it growth. Some businesses are already having = to make that call.

Cloudflare reports that more than half of inte= rnet traffic is now non-human. Yahia Bakour from context.dev shared that 55= % of his company's daily signups are agent-led. A business does not have to= be built for agents to find itself serving them.

We have seen this up close. We started getting= replies to our onboarding emails saying things like "No idea what this= is, my agent signed me up." Datadog contacted us about bulk agent sign= ups using agentmail.to addresses. Browser Use blocked signups from our shar= ed domain entirely, which applies equally to an abusive signup and a legiti= mate agent whose owner wants to try the product.

If a service sends an agent that signs up back= to its owner to sort out access, the work that was supposed to be delegate= d keeps landing back on the human. Getting this right determines how much w= ork people can actually hand over.


What the existing approa= ches solve, and where they stop.


CAPTCHAs and= bot filters detect whether a visitor is human. They do not establ= ish whose customer the agent is. A provider that wants to serve legitimate = agents still needs that relationship.

API keys solve access after setup. They do not help when an agent discovers a = service its owner has never used. Someone still has to establish the accoun= t.

Delegated ac= cess tools like Arcade and Auth0 Token Vault let an agent act thro= ugh an account you already have. They do not register the agent as a separa= te customer at a new service.

Web Bot Auth= verifies who sent the traffic. It still has to be mapped to an ac= count with an owner. Verifying the sender and creating that customer relati= onship are different parts of the job.


The human still matters. 

Businesses already understand this with teams.= Fifty employees can have fifty logins under one customer account. Each per= son gets appropriate access, and the supplier knows which business it is se= rving. Agents need an equally understandable relationship.

A provider should be able to recognize the ret= urning agent, connect other agents to the same owner, and contact that owne= r when a decision needs a person. That gives accountability a practical mea= ning. An owner's verified email gives you a contact. Identity gives them a = relationship they can act on.


What the right solution loo= ks like.


Imagine a sign-in option as familiar as "S= ign in with Google," designed for an agent arriving on its owner's beha= lf. The provider receives an identity it can recognize again and a connecti= on to the owner. The provider gives the agent its own account, sets permiss= ions and limits, and if another of your agents arrives, recognizes the shar= ed owner and applies its policy accordingly.

There is a commercial reason to get this right= . An agent evaluating your product may be choosing a tool its owner keeps u= sing. The easier you make legitimate access, the sooner it can find out whe= ther your product does the job.

That is what we are working toward. Building e= mail infrastructure for agents has put us in the conversations on both side= s: agents trying to get work done, and businesses trying to understand who = arrived. We have been building a way for agents to sign in as themselves, w= ith a connection to their human owners so tools and businesses can safely l= et their agents in. Stay tuned.

