                    <h2 style="font-size: 26px; font-weight: 700; line-he=
ight: 1.3; margin: 0 0 20px 0; color: #f0f0f0;">The Great Agent Sign-In Pro=
blem.</h2>

                    <p style="font-size: 15px; color: #cccccc; margin: 0 =
0 14px 0; line-height: 1.7;">Imagine 10,000 AI agents signing into your pro=
duct. Would you celebrate, or call your security team?</p>

                    <p style="font-size: 15px; color: #cccccc; margin: 0 =
0 14px 0; line-height: 1.7;">Those could be agents working for thousands of=
 customers who want to use your product. Or one person creating thousands o=
f accounts to burn through your free tier. You would want to know who was b=
ehind them before you called it growth. Some businesses are already having =
to make that call.</p>

                    <p style="font-size: 15px; color: #cccccc; margin: 0 =
0 14px 0; line-height: 1.7;">Cloudflare reports that more than half of inte=
rnet traffic is now non-human. Yahia Bakour from context.dev shared that 55=
% of his company's daily signups are agent-led. A business does not have to=
 be built for agents to find itself serving them.</p>

                    <p style="font-size: 15px; color: #cccccc; margin: 0 =
0 14px 0; line-height: 1.7;">We have seen this up close. We started getting=
 replies to our onboarding emails saying things like &quot;No idea what this=
 is, my agent signed me up.&quot; Datadog contacted us about bulk agent sign=
ups using agentmail.to addresses. Browser Use blocked signups from our shar=
ed domain entirely, which applies equally to an abusive signup and a legiti=
mate agent whose owner wants to try the product.</p>

                    <p style="font-size: 15px; color: #cccccc; margin: 0 =
0 14px 0; line-height: 1.7;">If a service sends an agent that signs up back=
 to its owner to sort out access, the work that was supposed to be delegate=
d keeps landing back on the human. Getting this right determines how much w=
ork people can actually hand over.</p>

                    <h3 style="font-size: 18px; font-weight: 700; color: =
#f0f0f0; margin: 24px 0 14px 0; line-height: 1.3;">What the existing approa=
ches solve, and where they stop.</h3>

                    <p style="font-size: 15px; color: #cccccc; margin: 0 =
0 10px 0; line-height: 1.7;"><strong style="color: #f0f0f0;">CAPTCHAs and=
 bot filters</strong> detect whether a visitor is human. They do not establ=
ish whose customer the agent is. A provider that wants to serve legitimate =
agents still needs that relationship.</p>

                    <p style="font-size: 15px; color: #cccccc; margin: 0 =
0 10px 0; line-height: 1.7;"><strong style="color: #f0f0f0;">API keys</st=> solve access after setup. They do not help when an agent discovers a =
service its owner has never used. Someone still has to establish the accoun=
t.</p>

                    <p style="font-size: 15px; color: #cccccc; margin: 0 =
0 10px 0; line-height: 1.7;"><strong style="color: #f0f0f0;">Delegated ac=
cess tools</strong> like Arcade and Auth0 Token Vault let an agent act thro=
ugh an account you already have. They do not register the agent as a separa=
te customer at a new service.</p>

                    <p style="font-size: 15px; color: #cccccc; margin: 0 =
0 20px 0; line-height: 1.7;"><strong style="color: #f0f0f0;">Web Bot Auth=
</strong> verifies who sent the traffic. It still has to be mapped to an ac=
count with an owner. Verifying the sender and creating that customer relati=
onship are different parts of the job.</p>

                    <h3 style="font-size: 18px; font-weight: 700; color: =
#f0f0f0; margin: 0 0 14px 0; line-height: 1.3;">The human still matters.</h=>

                    <p style="font-size: 15px; color: #cccccc; margin: 0 =
0 14px 0; line-height: 1.7;">Businesses already understand this with teams.=
 Fifty employees can have fifty logins under one customer account. Each per=
son gets appropriate access, and the supplier knows which business it is se=
rving. Agents need an equally understandable relationship.</p>

                    <p style="font-size: 15px; color: #cccccc; margin: 0 =
0 14px 0; line-height: 1.7;">A provider should be able to recognize the ret=
urning agent, connect other agents to the same owner, and contact that owne=
r when a decision needs a person. That gives accountability a practical mea=
ning. An owner's verified email gives you a contact. Identity gives them a =
relationship they can act on.</p>

                    <h3 style="font-size: 18px; font-weight: 700; color: =
#f0f0f0; margin: 0 0 14px 0; line-height: 1.3;">What the right solution loo=
ks like.</h3>

                    <p style="font-size: 15px; color: #cccccc; margin: 0 =
0 14px 0; line-height: 1.7;">Imagine a sign-in option as familiar as &quot;S=
ign in with Google,&quot; designed for an agent arriving on its owner's beha=
lf. The provider receives an identity it can recognize again and a connecti=
on to the owner. The provider gives the agent its own account, sets permiss=
ions and limits, and if another of your agents arrives, recognizes the shar=
ed owner and applies its policy accordingly.</p>

                    <p style="font-size: 15px; color: #cccccc; margin: 0 =
0 14px 0; line-height: 1.7;">There is a commercial reason to get this right=
. An agent evaluating your product may be choosing a tool its owner keeps u=
sing. The easier you make legitimate access, the sooner it can find out whe=
ther your product does the job.</p>

                    <p style="font-size: 15px; color: #cccccc; margin: 0 =
0 20px 0; line-height: 1.7;">That is what we are working toward. Building e=
mail infrastructure for agents has put us in the conversations on both side=
s: agents trying to get work done, and businesses trying to understand who =
arrived. We have been building a way for agents to sign in as themselves, w=
ith a connection to their human owners so tools and businesses can safely l=
et their agents in. Stay tuned.</p>

